// @description Drops QUIC handshakes, so HTTP/3 never starts and clients fall back to TCP.
// @patches nginx 1.25.0 to 1.31.8 with HTTP/3 enabled
// @cve CVE-2026-121810
// @severity high
// @tags http3, quic, nginx
// @knob port  The UDP port to guard.
//
// Fixed in nginx 1.31.9 and 1.30.7. Of use for any server whose HTTP/3 stack
// cannot be patched yet.
//
// One of the five nginx bugs disclosed in November 2026 is a use-after-free
// in its HTTP/3 code, reached by speaking QUIC to the server. The vendor's
// workaround is to turn HTTP/3 off. This does that from outside: a QUIC
// connection opens with long-header packets, and without them it never
// exists.
//
//   quic_handshake  a UDP datagram to the port whose first byte has the two
//                   top bits set: a QUIC long header (Initial, 0-RTT,
//                   Handshake, Retry), in version 1 or 2
//
// Checked against the Initial packet curl 8 sends with --http3-only
// (test/fixtures/quic-initial-curl.bin, first byte 0xcf). Other UDP on the
// port is left alone: DTLS and STUN open with a low byte, RTP with 10 in the
// top bits, not 11.
//
// A client told about HTTP/3 by an Alt-Svc header tries QUIC, gets no answer,
// and carries on over TCP; that fallback is how browsers behave and was not
// tested here. The first attempt costs it a timeout, so also stop advertising
// HTTP/3 (drop the Alt-Svc header, or `http3 off`) when you can.
//
// It drops rather than resets: UDP has no reset. TCP on the port, which is
// HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 over TLS, is not touched; for the HTTP/2 bugs disclosed
// with this one see http/h2c.pfc.
filter {
  if (proto != 17) decide pass;
  if (dport != knob("port") ?? 443) decide pass;
  if (len == 0) decide pass;
  if (u8(payload) & 0xc0 == 0xc0) decide drop("quic_handshake");
  decide pass;
}
